
Que:
- shell.py mixes `from src.configs import ...` with bare `from configs import ...` /
    `from utils import ...` (lazy imports inside methods). The bare form only resolves because
    setup.sh launches `python -m que.shell`/`que.server` from inside src/; it breaks under pytest
    from the repo root (tests/que/test_shell.py's TestSetMaxRuns has to prepend src/ to sys.path),
    and can load the same module twice under two names. Switch them all to `src.`-prefixed imports
    (core.py's were switched on 2026-10-02).
- Que reads (list_runs, peak_run, summarise_runs, len_sweep_runs, ...) don't take the Que's lock,
    only mutations do (see core._persists). The manager serves each connection on its own thread,
    so a read can interleave with a mutation from another client (e.g. the shell listing while the
    worker moves a run). Consider locking reads too.
- Que.load_state only handles FileNotFoundError: an unparsable or invalid Runs.json (e.g. hand
    edited) raises from Que(), so the server fails to start. Saves are atomic now, so it can't be
    truncated by a crash, but consider a clear error/fallback to the latest timestamped copy.
- tmux.py has 8 ruff warnings (unsorted imports, Optional[...] instead of `X | None`); all
    auto-fixable with `ruff check --fix src/que/tmux.py`.

Shell:
- `save que -t` writes a timestamped copy of Runs.json, but `save server` takes no options, so
    the server state can't be snapshotted the same way. ServerContext.save_state already takes
    out_path/timestamp; ServerContextProtocol.save_state just doesn't declare them, and the save
    parser's "server" subparser has no -t/-o flags. `load server` likewise can't load from
    another path (ServerContext.load_state takes in_path). Snapshotting both together (one -t
    for a matching Runs_<ts>.json + Server_<ts>.json pair, as made by hand on 2026-10-02) would
    help after incidents.
- add more flexibility to run summarisation
- add a flag to turn into debugging mode. Check debugging mode in unwrap_exception
- fix argument parsing short flags
- nargs="+" options swallow following positionals: e.g. `edit to_run 0 -ek acc 0.99` fails
    with "required: value" because -ek/--edit_keys consumes 0.99; the value has to be written
    before the flag (`edit to_run 0 0.99 -ek acc`). -c/--criterion has the same problem, so it
    must come last. Affects any parser mixing nargs="+" options with trailing positionals.
- indirect indexing is racy: edit/display/copy/to_config resolve the filtered/sorted index
    against a client-side snapshot (list_runs), then act on the server by original index in a
    separate call. If the queue changes in between (e.g. the worker pops to_run[0]), edit_run
    silently edits a different run (it re-validates fine, so nothing flags it). Possible fix:
    pass the expected run to edit_run and raise if peak_run(loc, idx) differs (note
    debug.any_dups -- content comparison only works if runs are unique).
- `server status` can't tell an idle daemon (awake, no work, polling -- see Daemon._idle) from one
    running a worker except by the worker's task. Consider an explicit idle flag in
    DaemonStateDict.

Daemon:
- Add better checking when starting daemon/worker (check it is actually working instead of just reporting
    that the process started successfully)

Worker:
- if GPU is busy on startup, gets stuck, needs server process to be restarted
- broader: train/sweep/test's outer `except Exception` blocks (worker.py's _fail path)
    still blindly stash/report every non-QueException the same way -- e.g. no distinction
    between a real crash and GPU OOM. Need to distinguish exception types explicitly instead
    of blanket `except Exception` + str(e) throughout worker.py/training.py. See also
    src/TODO.md's repo-wide blind-except audit item.

Server:
- `server load` at runtime still restores `awake` from the file: loading awake=True starts a
    supervisor, and awake=False while a supervisor runs makes status misreport it. Either keep
    the live `awake` value when the server is already up (only restore it at startup, for
    power-outage recovery) or refuse to load while the daemon is running.
- wandb's own per-run "Logs" tab / local output.log has been empty for a long time --
    NOT a block-scoping bug in the `redirect_stdout(self.log_adapter)` calls in
    _train/_sweep_train (tried merging the setup/training blocks into one continuous
    redirect on 2026-09-18; verified on a live debug-sweep trial that this made no
    difference -- output.log stayed empty, reverted). Real mechanism: wandb's
    wandb/sdk/lib/console_capture.py patches the *write method* of whichever object is
    sys.stdout at the first `import wandb` in the process, once, permanently ("not
    possible to unpatch or repatch" per its own docstring). Our redirect_stdout(...)
    reassigns the sys.stdout *name* to self.log_adapter during training, so print()
    calls self.log_adapter.write() directly and never touch wandb's patched object at
    all -- no restructuring of our `with` blocks changes that. Real fix needs to either
    (a) not reassign sys.stdout during training at all (which likely means moving
    training.py's print()-based step/epoch logging to a real logger, removing the need
    for redirect_stdout here in the first place -- training.py's bare print() calls are
    the underlying quick-and-dirty pattern), or (b) additionally register
    self.log_adapter's write via wandb.sdk.lib.console_capture.capture_stdout(...)
    directly so both wandb's capture and our own log file see the same writes (couples
    us to a wandb-internal module path, so needs a version-pin/compat note if taken).
- redirect testing output to log file

Files & logging:
- data and log files live beside the code in src/que/: Runs.json, Server.json, Server.log,
    Training.log, timestamped snapshots (Runs_<ts>.json/Server_<ts>.json, some git-tracked) and
    old_ques/. Move them to their own (gitignored) directory, e.g. src/que/state/ and
    src/que/logs/. Every path comes from the constants at the top of core.py (RUN_PATH,
    SERVER_STATE_PATH, SERVER_LOG_PATH, TRAINING_LOG_PATH, ARCHIVE_DIR), so the code change is
    small; the existing files need moving on the server (with the service stopped) and
    .gitignore/README updating. Decide whether the tracked snapshots stay in git.
- importing src.que.core runs logging.basicConfig (QUE_LOGGER = _get_basic_logger()), which
    points the root logger at Server.log as an import side effect. src/results/__init__.py imports
    it, so results notebooks/scripts can log into the live server's log; tests have to work
    around it (see CLAUDE.md's Testing section). Configure logging only in entry points
    (server.py, shell.py), and give Que a NullHandler-style default logger.
- logging setup is duplicated: ServerContext._setup_logging (basicConfig) and copy-pasted
    _reattach_server_logger methods in daemon.py and worker.py (plus worker's
    _attach_training_loggers). One helper (logger name, path, level -> logger) would do for all.
- no log rotation: on 2026-10-02 Training.log was 26 MB and Server.log 1.7 MB, growing forever.
    Several processes (server, supervisor, worker) append to Server.log through separate
    FileHandlers, so RotatingFileHandler isn't safe across them -- use logrotate (copytruncate)
    via setup.sh, or have child processes send records to the server (QueueHandler/socket).
- the `logs` parser's --worker help says "Tail the Worker.log file", but it tails Training.log
    (TRAINING_LOG_PATH). The `logs` command also reads these paths on the shell's machine, which
    isn't the server when the shell connects over the SSH tunnel.
